**1.6. "Derivative Work"**
Any work, in any form or medium, that:
 - (a) Is based upon, incorporates, adapts, or is derived from the Software in whole or in part;
 - (b) Translates, ports, converts, or transpiles the Software to another programming language or platform;
 - (c) Refactors, reorganizes, optimizes, or restructures the Software while retaining substantially similar functionality;
 - (d) Extends, enhances, wraps, or builds upon the Software's architecture, algorithms, or methodologies;
 - (e) Implements, instantiates, simulates, or replicates the Software's core logic or approach;
 - (f) Creates "clean room" implementations based on the Software's specifications or behavior;
 - (g) Combines the Software with other code, libraries, or systems to create integrated works;
 - (h) Generates outputs, models, or artifacts using the Software as a tool or substrate; or
 - (i) Would constitute a derivative work under 17 U.S.C. § 101 or applicable copyright law.

**This definition explicitly covers attempts to recreate functionality while avoiding literal code copying.**

**1.7. "Prohibited Party"**
Any individual or entity specifically identified in Article II, Section 2.3, or who meets the financial thresholds in Article II, Section 2.2.

**1.8. "Artificial Intelligence" / "AI"**
Any system, algorithm, or technology designed to simulate, approximate, or perform cognitive tasks, including but not limited to:
